  "id": "Compound::DB00935 - Gene::3356",
  "verbalization": "Oxymetazoline, a nasal decongestant, is shown to bind to Htr1b, a receptor involved in serotonin signaling, and Htr1b is part of the serotonin receptor family that is also engaged by Htr2a. This suggests a potential mechanism where Oxymetazoline may influence Htr2a through the activation of serotonin receptors. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Oxymetazoline interacts with Htr1b, which in turn is involved in the participation of Htr2a in serotonin receptor activity. The shared pathway from Oxymetazoline to Htr1b to Htr2a suggests a possible mechanism by which Oxymetazoline could modulate Htr2a function, potentially through serotonin signaling. The integration of these paths supports a mechanistic link between Oxymetazoline and Htr2a, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and functional role of Htr2a in this context.",
